ery high range, and while it may have some indirect correlation with other variables like Income or Ethnicity, it is unlikely to provide direct value. This column may introduce noise or overfit the model.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column BankCustomer is dropped\n# The feature BankCustomer has a high mean value (0.744) with low variance (Std = 0.4370) and overlaps in information with other features like Income or Debt. It may not provide additional predictive power.\ndf.drop(columns=['BankCustomer'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n--- \n\nReasoning for Selected Steps:\n\n1. Debt-to-Income Ratio is a well-known financial indicator and commonly used in credit risk evaluation, aligning with real-world decision-making processes.\n2. Employment Stability Bucket makes the age of employment more interpretable and highlights key thresholds, providing simplified group distinctions for the model to leverage.\n3. Citizen & Employment Cross captures potentially nonlinear interactions between features that could strongly indicate creditworthiness.\n\nThese features are intuitive and grounded in financial practices, helping the downstream model improve its predictions.", "python\n# Feature 1: Debt-to-Income Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of a person's outstanding debt to their income. Calculated as Debt / (Income + 1) to avoid division by zero.\n# Usefulness: Provides a measure of financial burden relative to earning ability, an important factor in creditworthiness. People with lower Debt-to-Income ratios may be more likely to qualify for a credit card.\ndf['Debt_to_Income'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 1)\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 2: Employment Stability\n# Feature description: Combined metric of YearsEmployed and Employed status. Creates a weighted measure that accounts for both employment duration and current employment. Calculated as (YearsEmployed + 1) * Employed.\n# Usefulness: Stability and continuity in employment are key indicators of financial stability. This feature helps capture both dimensions in a single value.\ndf['Employment_Stability'] = (df['YearsEmployed'] + 1) * df['Employed']\nend\n\npython\n# Feature 3: Priors and Defaults Interaction\n# Feature description: Indicates whether someone has a prior default and currently has a low credit score. Calculated as PriorDefault * (CreditScore < 2), where (CreditScore < 2) is converted to 0 or 1.\n# Usefulness: Having a prior default combined with a poor credit score significantly increases the risk profile of an applicant. This feature captures that relationship explicitly.\ndf['Priors_and_Defaults'] = df['PriorDefault'] * (df['CreditScore'] < 2).astype(int)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'ZipCode' is dropped\n# ZipCode is dropped as it likely doesn't contain predictive value for credit card eligibility. It is typically too granular and may not correlate strongly with other features.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'DriversLicense' is dropped\n# DriversLicense is dropped because its predictive power may be limited and redundant. Employment status and income likely provide stronger indications of financial stability than having a driver's license.\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column 'Citizen' is dropped\n# Citizen is dropped as it may not have a significant direct impact on credit card application success. Models already use other socioeconomic features like Income, Employment Stability, and Debt-to-Income ratio.\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\nend"]###,
